17:52:59 INFO catmaster.runtime.artifact_callback | [llm.context.message] model=openai/gpt-5.2:online callback_run_id=019cadf7-1cb4-74a2-b14e-6de9eae3ebe4 batch=0 idx=0 class=SystemMessage type=system payload={"content": "You are the Director of the Standard lane (dynamic execution controller).\n\nYou may use helper tools for read/check inspection before deciding.\nHelper tool available in this stage:\n- `bash_exec`\n\nInputs you will receive (in context message):\n- User request\n- Current proposal (markdown) + work_packages order\n- Memory index (autoload excerpt)\n- AlreadyDone: summaries of completed tasks\n- Available tools for task runner\n\nAllowed states:\n- PerformNextTask: dispatch one concrete next worker action with minimal scope creep.\n- MinorReviseProposal: small/local edits that keep the same route.\n- MajorReviseProposal: route-level change is required.\n- StopAndSynthesize: execution is complete or user asked planning-only output.\n\nDecision semantics:\n- Default priority: PerformNextTask > MinorReviseProposal > MajorReviseProposal.\n- Do not choose MajorReviseProposal when safe defaults/local edits can keep route valid.\n- If worker reports remote job failures, default to MajorReviseProposal and rerun only failed subset.\n- Do not treat proposal-format requirements as Director completion criteria.\n\nRules:\n- Avoid repeating completed work; consult AlreadyDone + memory index.\n- If the latest completed task already produced the user-requested final deliverables and there are no unresolved open questions, do not run additional verification passes; return `StopAndSynthesize` immediately.\n- Do not reread the same evidence file more than once unless the previous read failed or a different missing field still requires that file.\n- Never ask the worker to read metadata/internal run paths.\n- Never ask the worker to edit `MEMORY/**` or call `memory_apply_aider_edits`.\n- Preserve key parameters from proposal/default tables as suggested defaults and ask worker to follow them when feasible; allow bounded adjustment when needed to satisfy scientific invariants and done criteria.\n- Assume runtime environment is correctly configured per project README; do not escalate runtime/tooling prerequisites as human-blocking by default.\n- Do not revise or ask for confirmation for minor execution details; apply safe defaults and continue.\n- Do not invent file paths, tool outputs, or numerical results that are not evidenced by the run context.\n- If proposal has unresolved BLOCKING items, use MajorReviseProposal with updated proposal/work packages and concise HITL questions.\n", "additional_kwargs": {}, "response_metadata": {}, "type": "system", "name": null, "id": null, "__class__": "SystemMessage", "__type__": "system"}
17:52:59 INFO catmaster.runtime.artifact_callback | [llm.context.message] model=openai/gpt-5.2:online callback_run_id=019cadf7-1cb4-74a2-b14e-6de9eae3ebe4 batch=0 idx=1 class=HumanMessage type=human payload={"content": "User request:\nCompute O2 in a box: prepare VASP inputs from scratch, DO perform VASP calculation to get the results, and report final energy per atom and O–O bond distance from vasp results.\n\nProposal:\n# Proposal: O₂ in a cubic box (VASP) — inputs → run → extract energy & bond length\n\n## Items needing human decision\n- No blocking decisions needed. I will proceed with standard, defensible defaults for an isolated O₂ molecule in vacuum.\n\n## Key defaults / parameters (with rationale + confidence)\n- **Molecule / spin state:** O₂ (triplet ground state). Set **ISPIN=2** and **NUPDOWN=2** to target the triplet. *High confidence this improves robustness vs unconstrained spin.*\n- **Cell / vacuum:** cubic box via padding **10 Å** (≈ **20 Å** lattice). Minimizes image–image interaction for a neutral molecule. *High confidence.*\n- **DFT settings baseline:** MPRelaxSet (PBE, **ENCUT=520 eV**, **PREC=Accurate**, **LASPH=.TRUE.**, etc.) from the `vasp_relax_prepare` tool. *High confidence / widely used default.*\n- **Gas-phase preset:** `calc_type='gas'` → **Γ-only (1×1×1)**, **ISYM=0**, **LREAL=False**, **ISIF=2**. *High confidence; avoids symmetry/spurious constraints and improves accuracy for molecules.*\n- **Ionic relaxation:** `IBRION=2`, `NSW≈100–200`, `EDIFF=1e-6`, `EDIFFG=-0.01 to -0.02 eV/Å`. Default tool already sets tight EDIFF and EDIFFG=-0.02; keep unless convergence issues. *High confidence.*\n- **Dispersion:** **off** (no D3) since it is a single isolated molecule in vacuum. *High confidence.*\n\n## Execution strategy (grounded in available tools)\nWe will create an initial O₂ geometry, prepare VASP relaxation inputs using the “gas” preset, run VASP, then parse VASP outputs for:\n1) **Final total energy** and **energy per atom** (=E/2)\n2) **Final O–O bond length** from the relaxed geometry (CONTCAR or equivalent)\n\n## Planned workspace layout (paths)\n- `structures/o2_box/` — initial structure\n- `vasp_inputs/o2_gas_relax/` — VASP input deck prepared by tool\n- `vasp_runs/o2_gas_relax/` — VASP outputs from execution\n- `analysis/o2_gas_relax/` — parsed summaries (JSON/TXT)\n- Update memory pointers under `MEMORY/topics/{FILES,FACTS}.md` once results exist.\n\n## Validation / acceptance checks\n- Relaxation reaches force criterion (max |F| ≤ **0.02 eV/Å** unless tightened).\n- No obvious SCF failures (electronic convergence reached each step, no exploding energies).\n- Final O–O bond length reasonable (order ~1.2 Å under PBE for triplet O₂; exact value to be reported from the run).\n\n## Output to report (from VASP results)\n- **Final energy per atom (eV/atom)** from final ionic step energy / 2.\n- **Final O–O bond distance (Å)** from relaxed coordinates.\n- Provide provenance: which output file(s) and which final step was used.\n\n## Notes on reproducibility\n- Results depend on the exact POTCAR (PBE54 vs other) and VASP version; we’ll record these from OUTCAR headers when present.\n\n\nWork packages (ordered advisory milestones, not a fixed task script):\n[\"Generate initial O₂ structure in a sufficiently large cubic box (POSCAR)\", \"Prepare VASP gas-phase relaxation inputs (INCAR/KPOINTS/POTCAR) with triplet spin targeting\", \"Execute the VASP relaxation and confirm convergence/termination cleanly\", \"Parse VASP outputs to extract final total energy, energy per atom, and final O–O bond distance; write a small summary artifact\", \"Record final results and key run metadata into MEMORY (FACTS/FILES)\"]\n\nMemory index (autoload excerpt):\n# MEMORY (AUTOLOADED INDEX)\n\n## What this is\n- Autoloaded memory index for this project.\n- Keep this file concise and pointer-first.\n- Put details in MEMORY/topics/*.md.\n\n## Latest State\n- Current focus: Compute isolated triplet O2 in a cubic vacuum box using VASP and report final energy per atom and O–O bond distance from relaxed outputs.\n- Current milestone: Execution plan approved and committed to memory (task: `plan_commit`, run: `run_20260302_095018_3a4d0c`).\n- Latest run artifact: Plan/protocol invariants recorded in `MEMORY/topics/FACTS.md` (no result artifact yet).\n- Next action: Generate initial O2 structure in a sufficiently large cubic box, then prepare gas-phase relaxation inputs.\n\n## Top Constraints\n1. Final report must include energy per atom (eV/atom) and O–O bond distance (Å) from final relaxed VASP step with provenance.\n\n## Active Open Questions\n1. (empty)\n\n## Pointers\n- Goal / principles: MEMORY/topics/GOAL.md\n- Facts / decisions: MEMORY/topics/FACTS.md\n- Files / artifacts: MEMORY/topics/FILES.md\n- Constraints: MEMORY/topics/CONSTRAINTS.md\n- Open questions: MEMORY/topics/QUESTIONS.md\n- Runbook: MEMORY/topics/RUNBOOK.md\n\n## Keyword Map\n- GOAL: objective, success criteria, scope, non-goals\n- FACTS: definition, verified, result, decision\n- FILES: path, artifact, dataset, output, script\n- CONSTRAINTS: must, cannot, policy, budget\n- QUESTIONS: unknown, blocked, clarify, decide\n- RUNBOOK: steps, checklist, verify\n\n\nAlreadyDone (sanitized summary; metadata/internal paths omitted):\n[]\n\nTask status board (structured task history with outcomes):\n[]\n\nAvailable tools for task runner:\ncreate_molecule_from_smiles : \n    Build a 3D molecule from a SMILES string using RDKit. Specially, pay attention to the formal charge of the SMILES string, RDkit will check the charge and add Hs to the molecule to make it neutral.\n    \n\nmace_relax_batch : Submit multiple MACE relaxations in one DPDispatcher submission. Preferred tool for batch MACE relaxations.\n\nmace_sp_batch : Run MACE single-point jobs for structures under one directory.\n\nvasp_relax_prepare : \n    Prepare MPRelaxSet-based VASP relax inputs with calc_type presets and k_product k-mesh.\n    Also can be modified for other calculation types by overriding INCAR via the user_incar_settings.\n    Support exts: .vasp, .cif\n\n    Baseline DFT settings follow Materials Project MPRelaxSet (key defaults):\n    - PBE54 POTCARS, ENCUT=520 eV, PREC=Accurate, ISPIN=2, IBRION=2, LREAL=AUTO, LASPH=True, LWAVE=False, LCHARG=False.\n    - Additional overrides applied based on calc_type: ISMEAR=0, SIGMA=0.1 for bulk/slab; ISMEAR=0, SIGMA=0.01 for gas.\n    - ISIF policy: bulk with relax_cell=False -> ISIF=2; bulk with relax_cell=True -> ISIF=3.\n      slab and gas always use ISIF=2, where slab forces KPOINTS z=1 and gas forces KPOINTS 1x1x1 with ISYM=0, LREAL=False.\n    - Improved convergence settings: EDIFF=1e-6; NSW=500; EDIFFG=-0.02;\n    - LORBIT is controlled by compute_dos (default False -> LORBIT=0, True -> LORBIT=11).\n    - KPOINTS is generated by k*a=k_product, where a is the lattice constant in Angstrom. Set to 30 by default and sufficient for most systems.\n    - DFT-D3 and DFT+U are disabled by default. Enable them if the system is adsorption system(DFT-D3) or transition metal oxide system(DFT+U).\n    \n\nvasp_sp_prepare : Prepare MPRelaxSet-based VASP single-point inputs for structures under a file or directory. Parameters are the same as VaspRelaxPrepareInput, but with single_point relaxation enabled (NSW=0, IBRION=-1).\n\nbuild_slab : Build slabs for all terminations of a Miller index from bulk structure(s).\n\n    Provide exactly one of bulk_structure or bulk_dir. When bulk_dir is used, output_root is required.\n    Batch outputs are written under output_root/<slab_id>/..., where slab_id encodes the relative input path\n    (without suffix) using '__'.\n    \n\nfix_atoms_by_layers : Fix (freeze) the bottom N atomic layers of slab structure(s).\n\n    Provide exactly one of structure_ref or structure_dir. When structure_dir is used, output_dir is required.\n    Batch outputs are written to output_dir/<slab_id>.vasp and a summary JSON is written to\n    output_dir/batch_fix_atoms_by_layers.json.\n    \n\nfix_atoms_by_height : Fix (freeze) atoms within specified z ranges of slab structure(s).\n\n    Provide exactly one of structure_ref or structure_dir. When structure_dir is used, output_dir is required.\n    Batch outputs are written to output_dir/<slab_id>.vasp and a summary JSON is written to\n    output_dir/batch_fix_atoms_by_height.json.\n    \n\nsupercell : Create supercells from structure file(s). THIS WILL LOSE ALL SELECTIVE DYNAMICS INFORMATION.\n    Provide exactly one of structure_file or structure_dir. When structure_dir is used, output_dir is required.\n    Batch outputs are written to output_dir/<structure_id>.vasp and a summary JSON is written to\n    output_dir/batch_supercell.json.\n\n    The JSON format is:\n    {\n      \"results\": [\n        {\"input_rel\": \"a/CO.vasp\", \"structure_id\": \"a__CO\", \"output_rel\": \"out/a__CO.vasp\", \"natoms\": 2}\n      ]\n    }\n\n    \n\nenumerate_adsorption_sites : \n    Enumerate adsorption sites on a slab using ASF. Use it only for small scale placement.\n\n    The tool writes a JSON list to output_json:\n    [\n      {\"label\": \"ontop_0\", \"kind\": \"ontop\", \"cart_coords\": [x, y, z]}\n    ]\n    The returned tool data includes the workspace-relative path in \"sites\" and \"sites_json_rel\".\n    \n\nplace_adsorbate : Place an adsorbate molecule on a slab. Use it only if for small scale placement.\n    This tool keeps selective dynamics of the original slab structure and allows adsorbate to move freely.\n    It writes metadata sidecar <output>.meta.json and an ads_indices index JSON.\n\ngenerate_batch_adsorption_structures : \n    Generate multiple adsorbed structures up to max_structures. Suitable for large scale placement.\n    This tool keeps selective dynamics of the original slab structure and allows adsorbate to move freely.\n    Input can be a single slab file (slab_file) or a directory of slab files (slab_dir).\n    When slab_dir is used, each slab gets its own subdirectory under output_dir. Max_structures applies per slab.\n    For nested slab_dir layouts, slab_id encodes the relative path (without suffix) using '__'.\n\n    The tool writes a JSON list to output_dir/batch_structures.json and a per-structure\n    metadata sidecar <output>.meta.json with ads_indices.\n    It also writes output_dir/ads_indices.json as an index file:\n    [\n      {\n        \"slab_file_rel\": \"slabs/fe111.vasp\",\n        \"slab_id\": \"fe111\",\n        \"label\": \"ontop_0\",\n        \"output_poscar_rel\": \"adsorption/batch/fe111/ontop_0.vasp\",\n        \"ads_indices_added\": [36],\n        \"ads_indices\": [36]\n      }\n    ]\n    The returned tool data replaces \"structures\" with the workspace-relative path to this JSON file.\n    \n\nmake_neb_geometry : \n    Generate NEB interpolation geometries in nebmake.pl style.\n\n    The tool validates that initial and final structures have the same element order, atom count,\n    and lattice (within tolerance). Images are written under output_dir/00..(NI+1) with output_filename.\n    Selective dynamics constraints are preserved if present in the input files (ASE handles constraints).\n    \n\nmake_neb_incar : \n    Generate a VTST NEB/CI-NEB INCAR based on a template INCAR.\n\n    The tool parses the template INCAR (ignores comments/blank lines, case-insensitive keys),\n    applies NEB overrides, and writes the final INCAR along with a patch JSON.\n    \n\nvasp_execute_batch : Submit VASP runs in one DPDispatcher submission (single-folder or recursive batch modes).\n\nmp_search_materials : \n    Search Materials Project with flexible criteria and write results to CSV.\n\n    Common criteria keys (pass via `criteria`):\n      - material_ids (list[str] or str)\n      - elements (list[str] or str)\n      - chemsys (str)\n      - formula (str)\n      - is_stable (bool)\n      - energy_above_hull ([min, max])\n      - formation_energy ([min, max])\n      - band_gap ([min, max])\n      - crystal_system (str)\n      - spacegroup_number / spacegroup_symbol\n      - num_sites ([min, max])\n      - density ([min, max])\n      - volume ([min, max])\n      - num_elements ([min, max])\n    Common fields for output rows (pass via `fields`):\n      material_id, formula_pretty (or formula), energy_above_hull, formation_energy_per_atom (or formation_energy),\n      band_gap, crystal_system, spacegroup_number, spacegroup_symbol, nsites (or num_sites), density, volume.\n    \n\nmp_download_structure : Download one or more structures from Materials Project into the workspace.\n\nbash_exec : \n    Execute a multi-line bash script inside the workspace (default) and return stdout/stderr.\n    Network access is disabled by default using Linux network namespaces (unshare).\n    Symbolic link operations are disabled; use copy/move operations instead.\n    Stdout/stderr are returned as-is and projection/offload is handled centrally.\n    Keep output short in scripts and print one-line summaries when possible.\n    \n\nmemory_apply_aider_edits : Apply Aider SEARCH/REPLACE edit blocks to memory files.\n", "additional_kwargs": {}, "response_metadata": {}, "type": "human", "name": null, "id": "f975e3f8-31a8-4443-9fe6-33f627b4bc4f", "__class__": "HumanMessage", "__type__": "human"}
